<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Danilo I, Prince of Montenegro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1826">1826</a>–<a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Danilo I was the ruler of Montenegro who in 1852 declined consecration as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and made himself secular prince, turning the prince-bishopric into a hereditary principality with Russian backing. He issued a general law code in 1855, and his brother Mirko defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at Grahovac in 1858. He was assassinated at Kotor in 1860 and succeeded by his nephew Nicholas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/danilo-i-prince-of-montenegro.jpg" alt="Danilo I, Prince of Montenegro" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anastas Jovanović · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knjaz_Danilo_Petrovi%C4%87,_talbotipija_Anastasa_Jovanovi%C4%87a_1852_-_1853._godine_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Danilo I Petrović-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petar-ii-petrovic-njegos">Njegoš</a> was born on 25 May 1826. When Petar II Petrović-Njegoš died, the Senate, under the influence of Đorđije Petrović, the wealthiest Montenegrin of the time, proclaimed Petar's elder brother Pero Tomov prince rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. In a brief struggle Pero lost to the much younger Danilo, who had more support among the people.</p><p>Danilo made peace between the Crmnica and Katunjani tribes, was recognised by all the clans except the Bjelopavlići, and travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, supposedly to be ordained as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. He returned in 1852 with the endorsement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-i-of-russia">Nicholas I of Russia</a> to be prince, taking Pero and his supporters by surprise.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>An assembly declared Montenegro a principality on 1 March 1852, and Danilo was the first secular prince, after centuries of theocratic rule. He planned to raise Montenegro to a kingdom, which his nephew Nicholas achieved in 1910. War with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> began in 1852, when the Porte claimed jurisdiction in Montenegro. Danilo and his elder brother, Voivode Mirko, defeated the Ottomans at Ostrog in 1853. On 1 May 1858 Mirko led a 7,500-strong army to victory at Grahovac, after which a commission of the powers demarcated the border between Montenegro and the Ottoman Empire.</p><p>Danilo looked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> for a military ally. Russia, defeated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> of 1854, could not support Montenegrin demands at the Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1856. Danilo then accepted financial help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, 200,000 francs a year, and many Montenegrins saw this as a betrayal of Russia. At home he ruled in an authoritarian manner.</p><p>His General Law of the Land of 1855 drew on Petar I's law and Montenegrin custom, and is considered the first national constitution in Montenegrin history. He also held the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> in 1855, which counted a population of 80,000. In 1856 he sent Mirko against the Kuči, who had refused the new tax. About 247 people were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Danilo married Darinka Kvekić on 12 January 1855, and they had a daughter, Olga. On 11 August 1860 Todor Kadić, of the Bjelopavlići clan, assassinated him as he boarded a ship at the port of Kotor. He died two days later. His nephew Nikola succeeded him as prince. The town of Danilovgrad is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Danilo I, Prince of Montenegro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Petar II died in 1851, his nephew <b>Danilo</b> refused ordination and in 1852, with Russian approval, proclaimed himself secular prince, creating the Principality of Montenegro.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-montenegro">Prince-Bishopric of Montenegro</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1852 <b>Danilo Petrović-Njegoš</b>, heir of the Prince-Bishopric of Montenegro, declined to become a bishop and proclaimed himself secular prince with Russian backing.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-montenegro">Principality of Montenegro</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Njeguši, in what is now <a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a>. Died in Kotor, in what is now <a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.43,18.81&amp;year=1848" aria-label="Njeguši on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5006 655 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Danilo I, Prince of Montenegro"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5456 925h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5456 925h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.43,18.81&amp;year=1848">What was here in 1848? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
